UID:sloan-foundation-book-grants-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261024T233710Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Alfred P. Sloan Foundation: Book Grants (Public Understan
 ding of Science and Technology)
DESCRIPTION:The one Sloan program that funds individuals directly: grants f
 or authors researching and writing nonfiction books that bring science and
  technology to a general audience. Over 200 authors supported since 1996. 
 Favoured topics include elucidating confusing or controversial scientific 
 subjects\, profiles of figures in science and technology\, science-technol
 ogy-art connections\, women and science\, and the culture and philosophy o
 f science. Start with a letter of inquiry to program director Doron Weber\
 ; full proposals are by invitation only. Reviewed three times a year: LOIs
  by 1 September (decision by 15 February)\, 1 January (by 31 May) and 1 Ma
 y (by 31 October). No application fee.\n\nOrganisation: Alfred P. Sloan Fo
 undation\nLocation: Remote / anywhere (US foundation\; grants paid directl
 y to authors or via a nonprofit host)\nAward: Typically up to $60\,000 per
  book grant\nDuration: Project-based (book research and writing)\n\nMore: 
 https://sloan.org/programs/public-understanding/books?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://sloan.org/programs/public-understanding/books?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote / anywhere (US foundation\; grants paid directly to authors
  or via a nonprofit host)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bulletin-editorial-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261024T233710Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists Editorial Fellowship 20
 26
DESCRIPTION:The Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists runs an Editorial Fellows
  Program for emerging leaders who can give its audience regular and expans
 ive coverage of the Bulletin's core issues\, the existential threats facin
 g humanity. TRACKS: two specialty tracks are open in this round\, biosecur
 ity and disruptive technology/AI. Fellows write for the Bulletin's editori
 al pages\, producing up to four published pieces over the year at USD 750 
 each\, with limited travel costs and occasional incidental expenses covere
 d on approval. WHO SHOULD APPLY: this is not a student programme. Applican
 ts need significant professional experience\, an established record of pub
 lishing quality work\, and recognition as an emerging leader in their fiel
 d\; undergraduate and graduate students may apply only if they bring subst
 antial writing experience and published work. No geographic or citizenship
  restriction is stated. HOW TO APPLY: email a resume\, a cover letter (the
  call stresses that the cover letter matters)\, and up to three writing sa
 mples\, with 'editorial fellow' as the subject line\, to the address given
  on the call page. Fellows are announced in September 2026. Deadline 28 Au
 gust 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists\nLocation: R
 emote. No geographic or citizenship restriction is stated.\nAward: USD 750
  per published piece\, for up to four pieces annually\, so up to USD 3\,00
 0. Limited travel costs and occasional incidental expenses are also covere
 d. No application fee.\nDuration: Annual fellowship\, built around up to f
 our published pieces\n\nMore: https://thebulletin.org/2026/08/apply-to-be-
 a-2026-bulletin-editorial-fellow/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
URL:https://thebulletin.org/2026/08/apply-to-be-a-2026-bulletin-editorial-f
 ellow/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote. No geographic or citizenship restriction is stated.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai-philosophy-competition-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261024T233710Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261031
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261101
SUMMARY:Deadline: AI Philosophy Competition: First Edition
DESCRIPTION:The AI Philosophy Competition\, organised by philosophers Zacha
 ry Goodsell and Elliott Thornley and funded by Forethought Research\, aims
  to gauge the philosophical capabilities of AI in a way transparent to the
  philosophical community. Entrants submit up to three original essays on a
 ny philosophical topic\; essays must be primarily AI-generated\, with some
  human guidance allowed\, and each entry must include a methodology report
  explaining how the essay was produced. Essays are capped at 6\,000 words 
 each and may not be previously published or under review elsewhere\; entra
 nts consent to online publication and may choose anonymity. Judging is bli
 nd to methodology and authorship and evaluates the quality and originality
  of the philosophical content (style is not graded)\; the judging committe
 e includes David Chalmers\, John Hawthorne\, Herman Cappelen\, Cian Dorr a
 nd other academic philosophers. PRIZES: USD 3\,000 / 2\,000 / 1\,000 for t
 he top three essays\, plus a USD 5\,000 creative methodology pool. Finalis
 ts announced early December 2026\, results end of January 2027. DEADLINE: 
 31 October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Zachary Goodsell and Elliott Thornley\, 
 funded by Forethought Research\nLocation: Online submission via OpenReview
  (preferred) or Google Forms\; open internationally.\nAward: USD 3\,000 fi
 rst prize\, USD 2\,000 second\, USD 1\,000 third\, plus a USD 5\,000 pool 
 for creative methodology distributed at the judges' discretion (USD 11\,00
 0 total). No entry fee.\nDuration: Finalists announced early December 2026
 \; results announced end of January 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.zacharygood
 sell.com/ai-philosophy-competition?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.zacharygoodsell.com/ai-philosophy-competition?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online submission via OpenReview (preferred) or Google Forms\; ope
 n internationally.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:briarpatch-ai-resistance-winter-2027-pitches@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20261024T233710Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260918
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260919
SUMMARY:Deadline: Briarpatch Magazine: AI Resistance Issue\, Winter 2027 Ca
 ll for Pitches
DESCRIPTION:Briarpatch\, the Canadian magazine of grassroots politics\, ded
 icates its Winter 2027 issue to AI Resistance and is paying for articles\,
  investigative reporting\, historical analysis\, photo essays\, timelines\
 , profiles\, reading lists and art. SUGGESTED ANGLES include Indigenous-le
 d and local resistance to data centres\, exploitation and outsourcing of l
 abour in LLM training\, AI's impact on work and union resistance\, data mi
 ning and data sovereignty\, environmental and climate impacts\, artists an
 d AI (crisis of creativity and ownership)\, the language and inevitability
  narratives of AI\, policing and the surveillance state\, AI and disinform
 ation\, and investigations into Big Tech lobbying and media influence in C
 anada. PITCH: outline what ground your contribution covers\, estimated wor
 d count\, and your relevant experience\; first-time Briarpatch writers sho
 uld add a brief writing sample. Send to pitch [at] briarpatchmagazine.com.
  Accepted first drafts are due 14 October 2026 with collaborative editing 
 until late November\; publication is not guaranteed. No pitch fee. Deadlin
 e 18 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Briarpatch Magazine\nLocation: Regin
 a\, Canada (remote pitching\; Canadian focus)\nAward: CAD 150 for profiles
 \, short essays\, book reviews and parting shots (under ~1\,500 words)\; C
 AD 250 for feature stories and photo essays\; CAD 350 for research-based a
 rticles and investigative reporting (2\,000-2\,500 words). No pitch fee.\n
 Duration: First drafts due 14 October 2026\, editing through late November
 \n\nMore: https://briarpatchmagazine.com/winter-2027-pitches?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://briarpatchmagazine.com/winter-2027-pitches?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Regina\, Canada (remote pitching\; Canadian focus)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:nieman-fellowship-class-of-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261024T233710Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261201
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261202
SUMMARY:Deadline: Nieman Fellowships at Harvard\, Class of 2028
DESCRIPTION:The Nieman Foundation brings around two dozen journalists to Ha
 rvard each year for two semesters of study\, reflection and community\, on
 e of the oldest and best-known midcareer journalism fellowships in the wor
 ld. Fellows design their own course of study\, audit classes across Harvar
 d's schools and take part in Nieman's own seminars and master classes\; ma
 ny use the year to deepen a beat such as technology\, AI accountability\, 
 science or press freedom. WHAT YOU GET: a USD 87\,500 stipend paid over th
 e nine-month academic year (2026-27 figure)\, plus allowances toward housi
 ng\, childcare and health insurance. WHO IS ELIGIBLE: working journalists\
 , staff or freelance\, in any medium\, with at least five years of full-ti
 me media experience\; open to applicants from any country. Freelancers may
  use a recent supervising editor for one of the three required recommendat
 ion letters. Special fellowships within the program include the Nieman-Ber
 kman Klein Fellowship in Journalism Innovation. TO APPLY: applications for
  the class of 2028 open in the first week of October 2026 and require two 
 essays (personal statement and study proposal)\, work samples and three re
 commendation letters. DEADLINE: 1 December 2026 for international applican
 ts\; 31 January 2027 for US citizens.\n\nOrganisation: Nieman Foundation f
 or Journalism at Harvard\nLocation: Cambridge\, Massachusetts\, USA (resid
 ential academic year at Harvard)\nAward: USD 87\,500 stipend paid over the
  nine-month academic year (2026-27 figure)\, plus additional allowances fo
 r housing\, childcare and health insurance. Fellows audit courses across H
 arvard and participate in Nieman seminars\, master classes and workshops. 
 No application fee.\nDuration: Two semesters in residence at Harvard\, aca
 demic year 2027-28\n\nMore: https://nieman.harvard.edu/fellowships/how-to-
 apply/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://nieman.harvard.edu/fellowships/how-to-apply/?utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cambridge\, Massachusetts\, USA (residential academic year at Harv
 ard)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stigler-center-journalists-in-residence-2027@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20261024T233710Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260920
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260921
SUMMARY:Deadline: Stigler Center: Journalists in Residence Program Spring 2
 027 (Chicago Booth)
DESCRIPTION:The Stigler Center at the University of Chicago Booth School of
  Business invites journalists from around the world to its Journalists in 
 Residence Program\, a roughly 12-week residency on the Hyde Park campus in
  spring 2027 focused on the interactions between politics\, business\, mar
 kets and the economy. Running since 2017\, the program has hosted journali
 sts from outlets including the BBC\, The New York Times and ABC News. WHAT
  YOU DO: audit three Chicago Booth classes\, participate actively in class
  discussions and complete required readings and assignments\, attend Stigl
 er Center seminars\, lectures\, conferences and other activities\, collabo
 rate with peers\, network with faculty and researchers\, and explore oppor
 tunities to write for the Center's ProMarket publication. WHAT YOU GET: US
 D 14\,000 stipend for living expenses\, economy-class airfare reimbursemen
 t\, SEVIS and visa application fee coverage\, health insurance through UCh
 icago\, housing guidance\, academic opportunities and a certificate upon c
 ompletion. Note that the stipend is subject to up to 14 percent tax withho
 lding for international participants and arrives after roughly the first m
 onth\, so you need funds to bridge that period. ELIGIBILITY: open to journ
 alists from all countries and media backgrounds\, including freelancers an
 d editors\, with some years of professional experience\, proficiency in En
 glish and a genuine interest in political economy and business reporting. 
 Successful applicants are notified by the end of December 2026. DEADLINE: 
 20 September 2026\, 11:59 pm Chicago time (CT).\n\nOrganisation: Stigler C
 enter\, University of Chicago Booth School of Business\nLocation: Universi
 ty of Chicago Booth School of Business\, Hyde Park campus\, Chicago\, USA.
  Open to journalists from all countries and media backgrounds.\nAward: USD
  14\,000 stipend to cover living expenses over the 12-week program\, plus 
 reimbursement of economy-class airfare to and from Chicago\, SEVIS and vis
 a application fees\, health insurance through UChicago\, and housing guida
 nce. The stipend is subject to up to 14 percent tax withholding for intern
 ational participants\, and you need sufficient funds for roughly the first
  month before the stipend arrives. No application fee.\nDuration: Approxim
 ately 12 weeks\, tentatively 15 March to 31 May 2027\n\nMore: https://www.
 chicagobooth.edu/research/stigler/education/journalists-in-residence-progr
 am?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.chicagobooth.edu/research/stigler/education/journalists-in-
 residence-program?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referra
 l
LOCATION:University of Chicago Booth School of Business\, Hyde Park campus\
 , Chicago\, USA. Open to journalists from all countries and media backgrou
 nds.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:open-notebook-early-career-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261024T233710Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261031
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261101
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Open Notebook Early-Career Fellowship 2027 (remote)
DESCRIPTION:The Open Notebook's paid fellowship for early-career science jo
 urnalists is open for its 2027 round. Fellows spend twelve months working 
 part-time and remotely with a personal mentor\, reporting and writing arti
 cles about the craft of science journalism that are published at The Open 
 Notebook\, one of the field's central craft resources. Eligibility: early-
 career science journalists with fewer than three years of regular professi
 onal science-writing experience\; applicants from anywhere in the world ar
 e welcome\, and application materials are in English. TO APPLY: submit the
  application form (linked from the program page) by 31 October 2026\, 11:5
 9 PM US Central time\; reference letters are due 7 November 2026. The fell
 owship year starts 9 February 2027.\n\nOrganisation: The Open Notebook\nLo
 cation: Remote\, worldwide. Application materials in English\; internation
